+{"type": "user", "message": {"content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_0182nL9Uv8QkY5k8wHAb5oe2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t# Synthetic refunds example\n2\t\n3\tThese resources support the tutorial \"Find the rule your auditors are really\n4\tusing\" and its companion page of commands in the documentation site. Copy this\n5\tdirectory from the installed `gnd` package; no source checkout, provider\n6\taccount, learner install or external service is needed.\n7\t\n8\tAll data is synthetic. The shop, its stores, its customers and its auditors are\n9\tinvented for this example. Nothing here describes a real organization's policy.\n10\t\n11\tThe fictional shop's written rule: a refund should not be more than 1000. Its\n12\tauditors marked one week of refunds from each of eight stores clean or flagged.\n13\tThe weeks are built so that one simple rule, \"no refund over 500\", separates the\n14\tflagged weeks from the clean ones, while the written rule catches only two of\n15\tthe four flagged weeks.\n16\t\n17\t| Resource | Purpose |\n18\t|---|---|\n19\t| `model.orm.yaml` | Refunds, customers, amounts and payment channels, plus the written rule as an advisory (deontic) constraint |\n20\t| `batches/*.yaml` | One week of refunds per store: north, south, east and west are clean; harbor, market, airport and station are flagged |\n21\t| `labels.json` | The auditors' verdicts, a class-mode `gnd-labels/1` manifest binding the model and every batch by SHA-256 |\n22\t| `stand_in_learner.py` | A small, deterministic, standard-library rule learner. It is the example's own program, not a GND command and not Popper |\n23\t| `learners/<target>/` | Real learner outputs and run records for the ILP bridge's adapters (Popper, ILASP, a language model, RIPPER), replayed by \"Compare the rule learners on real runs\" |\n24\t\n25\t`stand_in_learner.py EXPORT_DIR OUT_DIR` reads the export written by\n26\t`gnd export ilp --target neutral` and writes `learned.clauses.json` and\n27\t`learned.run